  "chapter": "Chapter 3. Insurance and Indemnification",
  "section": "3.5. Commercial General Liability (CGL)",
  "subsection": "E. Commercial General Liability (CGL) Insurance",
  "text": "[3:421] No accidental “occurrence”: Because termination of employment is an intentional act, all claims arising out of such termination are subject to the same coverage analysis discussed above (¶ 3:357); i.e., since the termination is not an accidental “occurrence,” there is generally no coverage under a CGL policy. [See Loyola Marymount Univ. v. Hartford Acc. & Indem. Co. (1990) 219 CA3d 1217, 1224-1225, 271 CR 528, 532; American Guarantee & Liab. Ins. Co. v. Vista Med. Supply (ND CA 1988) 699 F.Supp. 787, 790 (applying Calif. law)] • [3:422] If tort claims based on negligence (e.g., negligent infliction of emotional distress, negligent misrepresentation, etc.) are incidental to the termination, there is no coverage. Because courts have found that termination of employment is 7 not an accidental “occurrence” (see ¶ 3:357 ff.), unintended results of the termination are treated the same way. [Dyer v. Northbrook Property & Cas. Ins.",
